/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Hosea chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("hosea", [
{n:6, name:"Return and Be Healed", seal:{ref:"Hosea 6:6",verse:"For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."}, facts:["Did you know? Hosea 6:2 promises revival 'after two days' and raising up 'in the third day,' a hope Christians have long read as pointing to the resurrection. (Hosea 6:2)","Did you know? Jesus quoted Hosea 6:6 twice, in Matthew 9:13 and 12:7, to defend showing mercy to sinners and the hungry over rigid rule keeping. (Hosea 6:6)","Did you know? Hosea calls shaky faith 'a morning cloud' and 'early dew' (6:4): bright for an hour, then gone by noon. (Hosea 6:4)"], questions:[
{q:"The people urge one another, 'Come, and let us return unto the LORD.' What do they say He will do, since He has torn and smitten?", options:["Heal them and bind them up","Forget them forever","Strike them again","Send them into exile"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:1",verse:"Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up.", insight:"The people's own confession names the cure: the same God who wounded is the God who heals and binds up."},
{q:"Who speaks the invitation to 'return unto the LORD' in the opening of this chapter?", options:["The people themselves, calling to one another","Only the priests","King Jeroboam","The king of Assyria"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:1",verse:"Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up.", insight:"Repentance begins as a call the people give each other; turning back to God is a shared resolve, not a private wish."},
{q:"After how many days will the LORD revive the returning people?", options:["After two days","After one day","After seven days","After forty days"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:2",verse:"After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight.", insight:"Two days marks a short, certain wait: God's healing is near to the one who returns, not lost in the far future."},
{q:"On which day will the LORD raise them up, so they live in His sight?", options:["In the third day","In the seventh day","In the fortieth day","On the Sabbath"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:2",verse:"After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight.", insight:"The third day points to full restoration; to be raised up is to be brought back into God's own sight and presence."},
{q:"To what is the LORD's going forth compared?", options:["The morning, prepared and sure","The lightning, sudden and frightening","The winter, cold and silent","The earthquake, shaking the hills"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:3",verse:"Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the LORD: his going forth is prepared as the morning; and he shall come unto us as the rain, as the latter and former rain unto the earth.", insight:"His coming is prepared as the morning: certain, steady, and sure to break, for those who follow on to know Him."},
{q:"The LORD says He will come to His people as what?", options:["As the rain, the latter and former rain upon the earth","As the wind that breaks the cedars","As hail that smites the vines","As a cloud that hides the sun"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:3",verse:"Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the LORD: his going forth is prepared as the morning; and he shall come unto us as the rain, as the latter and former rain unto the earth.", insight:"Rain is life to parched land; God's coming to the repentant is pictured as refreshment and growth, not as storm."},
{q:"To what does the LORD compare the goodness of Ephraim and Judah?", options:["A morning cloud and the early dew that goeth away","A strong tower that cannot be moved","A deep well that never runs dry","A cedar rooted on the high hills"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:4",verse:"O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away.", insight:"Their goodness looks bright at sunrise and vanishes by noon; God names fair-weather faith for exactly what it is."},
{q:"What troubled question does the LORD put to Ephraim and Judah?", options:["O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee?","Who will deliver thee from my hand?","Where are the gods thou hast made?","Why hast thou built so many altars?"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:4",verse:"O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away.", insight:"God's question shows His grief, not His indifference; He is pained by faith that melts away like dew."},
{q:"By whom has the LORD 'hewed' His people, as a sculptor cuts stone?", options:["By the prophets","By the kings","By the merchants","By the Assyrians"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:5",verse:"Therefore have I hewed them by the prophets; I have slain them by the words of my mouth: and thy judgments are as the light that goeth forth.", insight:"Prophetic words do the hard cutting work; God shapes His people through the sharp edge of truth spoken plainly."},
{q:"What are the LORD's judgments said to be like?", options:["As the light that goeth forth","As a sealed book none can read","As a hidden pit in the path","As thunder heard far away"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:5",verse:"Therefore have I hewed them by the prophets; I have slain them by the words of my mouth: and thy judgments are as the light that goeth forth.", insight:"His judgments are not riddles; they go forth like light, clear and impossible to mistake."},
{q:"What does God say He desires, and not sacrifice?", options:["Mercy","Riches","Victories in battle","Many children"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:6",verse:"For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings.", insight:"God weighs the heart above the altar; kindness to the suffering counts more with Him than the costliest offering."},
{q:"What does God desire more than burnt offerings?", options:["The knowledge of God","Long journeys to the temple","Beautiful songs in worship","A great multitude of people"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:6",verse:"For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings.", insight:"Burnt offerings without knowledge are empty smoke; God wants Himself known, not merely served with rituals."},
{q:"How does verse 7 say the people have transgressed the covenant?", options:["Like men, they have broken it","By accident, without knowing","Only in their dreams","Through the fault of their enemies"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:7",verse:"But they like men have transgressed the covenant: there have they dealt treacherously against me.", insight:"Their covenant breaking follows the old human pattern; like men before them, they turned from a given promise."},
{q:"Where and against whom have they dealt treacherously?", options:["There, against Me, says the LORD","In Egypt, against Pharaoh","In Samaria, against the king","At Gilgal, against the priests"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:7",verse:"But they like men have transgressed the covenant: there have they dealt treacherously against me.", insight:"Treacherous dealing is personal here; the broken covenant was against the Lord Himself, not merely against rules."},
{q:"What city is named as a city of workers of iniquity, polluted with blood?", options:["Gilead","Jerusalem","Bethel","Dan"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:8",verse:"Gilead is a city of them that work iniquity, and is polluted with blood.", insight:"Gilead's name once meant healing balm; now God names it a place stained with blood, showing how far a people can fall."},
{q:"With what is Gilead said to be polluted?", options:["With blood","With idols of silver","With strange fire","With unclean bread"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:8",verse:"Gilead is a city of them that work iniquity, and is polluted with blood.", insight:"Blood pollution marked a land of murder; the verse ties the city's guilt to real violence, not mere bad religion."},
{q:"The company of priests is compared to what, because they murder in the way?", options:["Troops of robbers waiting for a man","Shepherds guarding the flock","Watchmen upon the walls","Builders laying a foundation"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:9",verse:"And as troops of robbers wait for a man, so the company of priests murder in the way by consent: for they commit lewdness.", insight:"Those set to lead worship had become highway killers; the verse shows corruption at its worst when it wears holy robes."},
{q:"What horrible thing has the LORD seen in the house of Israel?", options:["The whoredom of Ephraim; Israel is defiled","A famine that empties the land","A plague among the cattle","The walls of Samaria broken"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:10",verse:"I have seen an horrible thing in the house of Israel: there is the whoredom of Ephraim, Israel is defiled.", insight:"God calls spiritual unfaithfulness what it is, a horrible defilement, because He sees the whole house as one people."},
{q:"What does the LORD say He has set for Judah?", options:["An harvest","A famine","A new king","A foreign master"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:11",verse:"Also, O Judah, he hath set an harvest for thee, when I returned the captivity of my people.", insight:"Even in judgment an harvest is set; God's reckoning for Judah holds a season of return built into it."},
{q:"When will the harvest set for Judah come?", options:["When I returned the captivity of my people","When the temple is rebuilt","When the rains fail three years","When Egypt is conquered"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 6:11",verse:"Also, O Judah, he hath set an harvest for thee, when I returned the captivity of my people.", insight:"The harvest is tied to homecoming; God's restoring of His captive people is the season He has appointed."}
]},
{n:7, name:"The Heated Oven", seal:{ref:"Hosea 7:11",verse:"Ephraim also is like a silly dove without heart: they call to Egypt, they go to Assyria."}, facts:["Did you know? The 'cake not turned' of Hosea 7:8 is flatbread baked on hot stones and flipped; half burnt and half raw, it pictures a nation ruined by half measures. (Hosea 7:8)","Did you know? 'Gray hairs are here and there upon him, yet he knoweth not' (7:9): Israel was weakening and aging without even noticing it. (Hosea 7:9)","Did you know? The 'silly dove without heart' of 7:11 names Ephraim's foreign policy: fluttering to Egypt and Assyria for rescue instead of trusting God. (Hosea 7:11)"], questions:[
{q:"When the LORD would have healed Israel, what was discovered instead?", options:["The iniquity of Ephraim and the wickedness of Samaria","A hidden treasure in the temple","A conspiracy among the shepherds","The lost tables of the law"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:1",verse:"When I would have healed Israel, then the iniquity of Ephraim was discovered, and the wickedness of Samaria: for they commit falsehood; and the thief cometh in, and the troop of robbers spoileth without.", insight:"Healing was God's intent, but the sickness was laid bare first; God exposes the wound before He binds it."},
{q:"What do the people commit, according to the opening verse?", options:["Falsehood, while the thief comes in and robbers spoil without","Honest labor that earns them bread","Vows they keep with great care","Prayers that rise like incense"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:1",verse:"When I would have healed Israel, then the iniquity of Ephraim was discovered, and the wickedness of Samaria: for they commit falsehood; and the thief cometh in, and the troop of robbers spoileth without.", insight:"Lies at home and crime on every side mark a people whose heart is sick; God names both the inward lie and the outward theft."},
{q:"What do they not consider in their hearts?", options:["That God remembers all their wickedness","That the harvest is near","That the king is watching them","That the priests are praying"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:2",verse:"And they consider not in their hearts that I remember all their wickedness: now their own doings have beset them about; they are before my face.", insight:"Their blindness is inward; they act as if God forgets, while their own doings surround them and stand before His face."},
{q:"Whom do they make glad with their wickedness and their lies?", options:["The king and the princes","The poor and the needy","The widows and the orphans","The strangers in the land"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:3",verse:"They make the king glad with their wickedness, and the princes with their lies.", insight:"Wickedness had become entertainment for the rulers; a nation's leaders delighting in lies shows rot at the top."},
{q:"To what are the adulterers of Israel compared?", options:["An oven heated by the baker","A field left unplowed","A lamp with no oil","A bow without a string"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:4",verse:"They are all adulterers, as an oven heated by the baker, who ceaseth from raising after he hath kneaded the dough, until it be leavened.", insight:"Lust is pictured as a baker's oven, heated and waiting; the image shows passion deliberately stoked, not stumbled into."},
{q:"What happened 'in the day of our king'?", options:["The princes made him sick with bottles of wine","The people brought him gifts of gold","The priests anointed him with oil","The army won a great victory"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:5",verse:"In the day of our king the princes have made him sick with bottles of wine; he stretched out his hand with scorners.", insight:"The royal court ran on drunkenness and mockery; when leaders drown in wine, scorners set the tone of the nation."},
{q:"While they lie in wait, what are their hearts like?", options:["Like an oven, burning as a flaming fire in the morning","Like still water, calm and clear","Like a rock, firm and unmoved","Like a sealed chest, keeping secrets"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:6",verse:"For they have made ready their heart like an oven, whiles they lie in wait: their baker sleepeth all the night; in the morning it burneth as a flaming fire.", insight:"Scheming hearts are ovens kept hot all night; the verse shows plots simmering in the dark before they blaze at dawn."},
{q:"What have the overheated people devoured?", options:["Their judges, while all their kings are fallen","The bread of the temple","The flocks of the shepherds","The grain of the harvest"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:7",verse:"They are all hot as an oven, and have devoured their judges; all their kings are fallen: there is none among them that calleth unto me.", insight:"Political murder had become routine; they consumed their own rulers, yet none among them called out to God."},
{q:"Ephraim is pictured as what kind of cake?", options:["A cake not turned","A cake burned to ash","A cake shared with strangers","A cake baked for the king"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:8",verse:"Ephraim, he hath mixed himself among the people; Ephraim is a cake not turned.", insight:"Half baked and half raw, the unturned cake pictures a people half with God and half with the nations, good on neither side."},
{q:"What has Ephraim done among the peoples?", options:["Mixed himself among the people","Separated himself to holiness","Preached to the nations","Built walls around Samaria"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:8",verse:"Ephraim, he hath mixed himself among the people; Ephraim is a cake not turned.", insight:"Mixing with the nations meant borrowing their ways; Israel traded her distinct calling for the fashions of her neighbors."},
{q:"What have strangers devoured, without Ephraim knowing it?", options:["His strength","His harvests of wheat","His flocks and herds","His temple treasures"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:9",verse:"Strangers have devoured his strength, and he knoweth it not: yea, gray hairs are here and there upon him, yet he knoweth not.", insight:"Strength was draining away to foreigners while Israel felt nothing; decline is most dangerous when it goes unnoticed."},
{q:"What sign of aging is upon Ephraim, though he does not know it?", options:["Gray hairs are here and there upon him","His eyes have grown dim","His hands have lost their skill","His voice has become weak"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:9",verse:"Strangers have devoured his strength, and he knoweth it not: yea, gray hairs are here and there upon him, yet he knoweth not.", insight:"Gray hairs mark a nation growing old and weak; God says Israel was aging into ruin and could not even see it."},
{q:"What testifies to Israel's face, though they do not return to the LORD?", options:["The pride of Israel","The beauty of Samaria","The strength of the army","The wealth of the merchants"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:10",verse:"And the pride of Israel testifieth to his face: and they do not return to the LORD their God, nor seek him for all this.", insight:"Pride stood as a witness against them; the very arrogance that kept them from returning testified openly of their guilt."},
{q:"Ephraim is like what kind of dove?", options:["A silly dove without heart","A swift dove of the mountains","A mourning dove of the valleys","A white dove of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:11",verse:"Ephraim also is like a silly dove without heart: they call to Egypt, they go to Assyria.", insight:"A silly dove flutters anywhere for help; Ephraim's heartless panic sent her flapping between Egypt and Assyria instead of to God."},
{q:"To which two powers does the silly dove call for help?", options:["To Egypt and to Assyria","To Babylon and to Persia","To Tyre and to Sidon","To Moab and to Edom"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:11",verse:"Ephraim also is like a silly dove without heart: they call to Egypt, they go to Assyria.", insight:"Egypt and Assyria were rival empires, yet Israel begged both; faithless diplomacy chased every rescuer but the Lord."},
{q:"When they go to those powers, what will the LORD spread upon them?", options:["His net, to bring them down as the fowls of the heaven","His wings, to shelter them","His banner, to guide them","His table, to feed them"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:12",verse:"When they shall go, I will spread my net upon them; I will bring them down as the fowls of the heaven; I will chastise them, as their congregation hath heard.", insight:"The dove that fled God's hand flies into God's net; running to foreign powers only lands them in the judgment He foretold."},
{q:"Why is 'woe' pronounced upon them in verse 13?", options:["They have fled from Me and spoken lies against Me","They have built too many houses","They have forgotten the Sabbath","They have eaten unclean food"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:13",verse:"Woe unto them! for they have fled from me: destruction unto them! because they have transgressed against me: though I have redeemed them, yet they have spoken lies against me.", insight:"Though God redeemed them, they fled and lied about Him; woe falls on those who repay redemption with falsehood."},
{q:"How do they cry, and for what do they assemble?", options:["They howl upon their beds and assemble for corn and wine","They weep at the altar and assemble for prayer","They sing in the temple and assemble for feasts","They fast in sackcloth and assemble for mourning"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:14",verse:"And they have not cried unto me with their heart, when they howled upon their beds: they assemble themselves for corn and wine, and they rebel against me.", insight:"Their cries were for crops, not for God; howling for bread while rebelling against the Giver shows the hollowness of their worship."},
{q:"Though God bound and strengthened their arms, what did they do?", options:["They imagined mischief against Me","They built houses for the poor","They returned to keep the law","They sang songs of thanksgiving"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:15",verse:"Though I have bound and strengthened their arms, yet do they imagine mischief against me.", insight:"God gave them strength and they spent it plotting against Him; the verse shows kindness repaid with treachery."},
{q:"When they return, but not to the Most High, what are they like?", options:["Like a deceitful bow","Like a broken yoke","Like a dried up spring","Like a torn garment"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 7:16",verse:"They return, but not to the most High: they are like a deceitful bow: their princes shall fall by the sword for the rage of their tongue: this shall be their derision in the land of Egypt.", insight:"A deceitful bow looks ready but shoots crooked; their half returns aimed at everything except the Most High."}
]},
{n:8, name:"Sown the Wind", seal:{ref:"Hosea 8:7",verse:"For they have sown the wind, and they shall reap the whirlwind: it hath no stalk: the bud shall yield no meal: if so be it yield, the strangers shall swallow it up."}, facts:["Did you know? 'They have sown the wind, and they shall reap the whirlwind' (8:7) is one of the most quoted proverbs in the Bible about reaping what you sow. (Hosea 8:7)","Did you know? Israel set up kings 'but not by me' (8:4); in Hosea's own lifetime the northern throne changed hands by assassination after assassination. (Hosea 8:4)","Did you know? God says 'I have written to him the great things of my law, but they were counted as a strange thing' (8:12): Israel treated God's written word like a foreign language. (Hosea 8:12)"], questions:[
{q:"What is Hosea told to set to his mouth, as a warning of coming judgment?", options:["The trumpet","The flute","The harp","The timbrel"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:1",verse:"Set the trumpet to thy mouth. He shall come as an eagle against the house of the LORD, because they have transgressed my covenant, and trespassed against my law.", insight:"The trumpet called the people to alarm; judgment was announced openly, not sprung on them in secret."},
{q:"He shall come as what against the house of the LORD?", options:["As an eagle","As a lion","As a flood","As a fire"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:1",verse:"Set the trumpet to thy mouth. He shall come as an eagle against the house of the LORD, because they have transgressed my covenant, and trespassed against my law.", insight:"The eagle pictures a swift invader swooping down; the reason given is plain, they transgressed the covenant and the law."},
{q:"What will Israel cry to God when the enemy comes?", options:["My God, we know thee","Lord, we never sinned","Save us, we are innocent","Remember our sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:2",verse:"Israel shall cry unto me, My God, we know thee.", insight:"In danger they claim to know God, but the cry comes too late; acquaintance claimed in crisis is not the same as knowing Him in peace."},
{q:"What has Israel cast off?", options:["The thing that is good","The yoke of Egypt","The laws of Moses","The temple in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:3",verse:"Israel hath cast off the thing that is good: the enemy shall pursue him.", insight:"They threw away the good itself; the verse names the root loss, not just bad deeds but rejection of the good God gave."},
{q:"What will happen to the one who cast off the good?", options:["The enemy shall pursue him","He shall find rest","The land shall prosper","His name shall be honored"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:3",verse:"Israel hath cast off the thing that is good: the enemy shall pursue him.", insight:"Rejecting the good leaves a man unguarded; the enemy's pursuit follows naturally once God's protection is cast off."},
{q:"Of what did they make their idols?", options:["Of their silver and their gold","Of clay from the river","Of wood from the forest","Of stone from the hills"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:4",verse:"They have set up kings, but not by me: they have made princes, and I knew it not: of their silver and their gold have they made them idols, that they may be cut off.", insight:"Their wealth became their worship; the very silver and gold God gave were melted into gods that would cut them off."},
{q:"Whom did they set up without the LORD's choosing?", options:["Kings and princes, but not by Me","Priests and Levites","Prophets and judges","Captains and soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:4",verse:"They have set up kings, but not by me: they have made princes, and I knew it not: of their silver and their gold have they made them idols, that they may be cut off.", insight:"Self-made kings without God's appointment show a people governing themselves apart from the One who made them a nation."},
{q:"What does the LORD say of Samaria's calf?", options:["Thy calf, O Samaria, hath cast thee off","Thy calf shall protect thee forever","Thy calf is lovely in my sight","Thy calf shall stand in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:5",verse:"Thy calf, O Samaria, hath cast thee off; mine anger is kindled against them: how long will it be ere they attain to innocency?", insight:"The idol they trusted has cast them off; the verse turns their worship around, showing the calf abandons its makers."},
{q:"The calf of Samaria is the work of whom, and therefore what?", options:["The workman made it; therefore it is not God","The angels made it; therefore it is holy","Moses made it; therefore it is lawful","The priests made it; therefore it is blessed"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:6",verse:"For from Israel was it also: the workman made it; therefore it is not God: but the calf of Samaria shall be broken in pieces.", insight:"A god with a human maker is no god at all; the verse states the plain logic that idolatry hopes no one will notice."},
{q:"What will become of the calf of Samaria?", options:["It shall be broken in pieces","It shall be carried to Jerusalem","It shall be covered with gold","It shall stand forever"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:6",verse:"For from Israel was it also: the workman made it; therefore it is not God: but the calf of Samaria shall be broken in pieces.", insight:"Man-made gods end in fragments; what the workman built, judgment will break, proving it was never divine."},
{q:"What shall they reap who have sown the wind?", options:["The whirlwind","A gentle rain","An empty field","A double harvest"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:7",verse:"For they have sown the wind, and they shall reap the whirlwind: it hath no stalk: the bud shall yield no meal: if so be it yield, the strangers shall swallow it up.", insight:"Sowing wind reaps whirlwind: the harvest always outgrows the seed, and empty deeds return as devastating storms."},
{q:"Of the wind-sown crop it is said the bud shall yield what?", options:["No meal","A rich harvest","Bread for the poor","Seed for next year"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:7",verse:"For they have sown the wind, and they shall reap the whirlwind: it hath no stalk: the bud shall yield no meal: if so be it yield, the strangers shall swallow it up.", insight:"The crop has stalk without grain; their labor looked like farming but produced nothing worth eating."},
{q:"Among the Gentiles, Israel shall be as what?", options:["A vessel wherein is no pleasure","A lamp set on a hill","A banner lifted high","A city full of joy"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:8",verse:"Israel is swallowed up: now shall they be among the Gentiles as a vessel wherein is no pleasure.", insight:"Swallowed up and useless among the nations, Israel became a discarded vessel; exile turned the chosen people into nobodies."},
{q:"Where have they gone up, and like what creature?", options:["To Assyria, like a wild ass alone by himself","To Egypt, like a dove to its nest","To Babylon, like a lion to its den","To Tyre, like a ship to harbor"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:9",verse:"For they are gone up to Assyria, a wild ass alone by himself: Ephraim hath hired lovers.", insight:"The wild ass wanders stubbornly alone; Israel's lonely trek to Assyria showed a nation too proud to stay near God."},
{q:"What has Ephraim done among the nations?", options:["Hired lovers","Built fortresses","Planted vineyards","Dug wells"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:9",verse:"For they are gone up to Assyria, a wild ass alone by himself: Ephraim hath hired lovers.", insight:"Alliances were bought like favors; Ephraim paid foreign powers for protection that only God could truly give."},
{q:"They shall sorrow a little for what burden?", options:["For the burden of the king of princes","For the taxes of the temple","For the loss of the harvest","For the death of the king"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:10",verse:"Yea, though they have hired among the nations, now will I gather them, and they shall sorrow a little for the burden of the king of princes.", insight:"The hired alliances became a crushing tax; the king of princes, Assyria, would make them pay dearly for his favor."},
{q:"Ephraim made many altars for what purpose?", options:["To sin; altars shall be unto him to sin","To pray; altars shall be unto him to pray","To give thanks; altars shall be unto him to praise","To remember; altars shall be unto him to recall"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:11",verse:"Because Ephraim hath made many altars to sin, altars shall be unto him to sin.", insight:"More altars meant more sin, not more worship; the verse shows religion multiplied can simply multiply guilt."},
{q:"How did they treat the great things of God's law which He had written?", options:["They counted them as a strange thing","They memorized them with joy","They taught them to their children","They carved them in stone"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:12",verse:"I have written to him the great things of my law, but they were counted as a strange thing.", insight:"God's written word felt foreign to them; when Scripture seems strange, it reveals how far the heart has wandered."},
{q:"Why will the LORD not accept their sacrifices of flesh?", options:["He will remember their iniquity and visit their sins","Their meat was not properly cooked","They brought too little for the priests","The altar was built in the wrong place"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:13",verse:"They sacrifice flesh for the sacrifices of mine offerings, and eat it; but the LORD accepteth them not; now will he remember their iniquity, and visit their sins: they shall return to Egypt.", insight:"Sacrifice without obedience is refused; God says He will remember the sins the offerings were meant to cover."},
{q:"What has Israel forgotten, and what will God send?", options:["Forgotten his Maker; God will send a fire upon his cities","Forgotten his king; God will send a new ruler","Forgotten his name; God will send a prophet","Forgotten his land; God will send rain"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 8:14",verse:"For Israel hath forgotten his Maker, and buildeth temples; and Judah hath multiplied fenced cities: but I will send a fire upon his cities, and it shall devour the palaces thereof.", insight:"Forgetting the Maker while building temples and fenced cities ends in fire on those very cities; God will not be replaced by His gifts."}
]},
{n:9, name:"Wanderers Among the Nations", seal:{ref:"Hosea 9:17",verse:"My God will cast them away, because they did not hearken unto him: and they shall be wanderers among the nations."}, facts:["Did you know? Hosea 9:10 recalls Baal-peor, the shame of Numbers 25, when Israel joined herself to a Moabite idol; 'their abominations were according as they loved.' (Hosea 9:10)","Did you know? 'All their wickedness is in Gilgal' (9:15); Gilgal was where Israel first camped after crossing the Jordan, now turned into a shrine of idolatry. (Hosea 9:15)","Did you know? 'As for Ephraim, their glory shall fly away like a bird' (9:11): judgment is pictured as every blessing taking wing, from birth to womb to conception. (Hosea 9:11)"], questions:[
{q:"Why should Israel not rejoice for joy, as other people do?", options:["She has gone a whoring from her God","She has lost her harvest to locusts","Her king has been taken captive","Her walls have been broken down"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:1",verse:"Rejoice not, O Israel, for joy, as other people: for thou hast gone a whoring from thy God, thou hast loved a reward upon every cornfloor.", insight:"Joy belongs to the faithful; a people unfaithful to God is told plainly that celebration would be a lie."},
{q:"What has Israel loved, according to the opening verse?", options:["A reward upon every cornfloor","The statutes of the LORD","The counsel of the prophets","The peace of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:1",verse:"Rejoice not, O Israel, for joy, as other people: for thou hast gone a whoring from thy God, thou hast loved a reward upon every cornfloor.", insight:"She sold her love for harvest pay; the cornfloor, meant for God's provision, became the marketplace of her unfaithfulness."},
{q:"What shall happen to the floor, the winepress, and the new wine?", options:["They shall not feed them, and the new wine shall fail","They shall overflow with abundance","They shall be given to the priests","They shall be stored for seven years"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:2",verse:"The floor and the winepress shall not feed them, and the new wine shall fail in her.", insight:"The very harvest she sold herself for will fail; God strikes the false reward first, cutting off the wages of unfaithfulness."},
{q:"Where shall Ephraim return, and what shall they eat?", options:["To Egypt, and eat unclean things in Assyria","To Babylon, and eat the king's meat","To Moab, and eat bread in the wilderness","To Edom, and eat bitter herbs"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:3",verse:"They shall not dwell in the LORD’S land; but Ephraim shall return to Egypt, and they shall eat unclean things in Assyria.", insight:"They will not dwell in the LORD's land; exile reverses the Exodus, sending them back to Egypt and into defiled food."},
{q:"What shall their sacrifices be like in that day?", options:["As the bread of mourners; all that eat thereof shall be polluted","As a sweet savor rising to heaven","As a feast of joy for the people","As an offering the LORD accepts"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:4",verse:"They shall not offer wine offerings to the LORD, neither shall they be pleasing unto him: their sacrifices shall be unto them as the bread of mourners; all that eat thereof shall be polluted: for their bread for their soul shall not come into the house of the LORD.", insight:"Offerings become mourner's bread, unfit for God's house; worship performed in exile carries the pollution of the exile itself."},
{q:"What searching question is asked about the solemn day?", options:["What will ye do in the solemn day, and in the day of the feast of the LORD?","Who will lead the songs in the temple?","How many lambs shall ye bring?","Where will ye pitch your tents?"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:5",verse:"What will ye do in the solemn day, and in the day of the feast of the LORD?", insight:"Feasts without a land to keep them in become impossible questions; judgment takes away the very setting of their worship."},
{q:"Who shall gather them up, and what city shall bury them?", options:["Egypt shall gather them up; Memphis shall bury them","Assyria shall gather them up; Nineveh shall bury them","Babylon shall gather them up; Shushan shall bury them","Moab shall gather them up; Heshbon shall bury them"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:6",verse:"For, lo, they are gone because of destruction: Egypt shall gather them up, Memphis shall bury them: the pleasant places for their silver, nettles shall possess them: thorns shall be in their tabernacles.", insight:"Egypt, the old house of bondage, becomes their graveyard; Memphis burying them closes the circle of their flight from God."},
{q:"What shall possess their pleasant places for silver?", options:["Nettles, and thorns shall be in their tabernacles","Roses, and lilies in their courts","Olive trees, and vines on their walls","Cedars, and fir trees in their gates"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:6",verse:"For, lo, they are gone because of destruction: Egypt shall gather them up, Memphis shall bury them: the pleasant places for their silver, nettles shall possess them: thorns shall be in their tabernacles.", insight:"Weeds inherit the homes of wealth; nettles and thorns mark the ruin of every house built on unfaithfulness."},
{q:"What days are come, which Israel shall know?", options:["The days of visitation and the days of recompence","The days of harvest and the days of feasting","The days of peace and the days of rest","The days of building and the days of planting"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:7",verse:"The days of visitation are come, the days of recompence are come; Israel shall know it: the prophet is a fool, the spiritual man is mad, for the multitude of thine iniquity, and the great hatred.", insight:"Visitation and recompense mean accounts settled; Israel will know by experience what she refused to learn by warning."},
{q:"What is said of the prophet and the spiritual man in those days?", options:["The prophet is a fool, the spiritual man is mad","The prophet is honored, the spiritual man is praised","The prophet is silent, the spiritual man is hidden","The prophet is fled, the spiritual man is forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:7",verse:"The days of visitation are come, the days of recompence are come; Israel shall know it: the prophet is a fool, the spiritual man is mad, for the multitude of thine iniquity, and the great hatred.", insight:"Truth tellers are mocked as fools when iniquity multiplies; the verse shows how sin first corrupts a people's judgment of God's messengers."},
{q:"In all his ways, the false prophet is what?", options:["A snare of a fowler","A light to the path","A staff for the weak","A shield in battle"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:8",verse:"The watchman of Ephraim was with my God: but the prophet is a snare of a fowler in all his ways, and hatred in the house of his God.", insight:"The watchman turned trapper; a prophet who should warn has become a snare, catching souls instead of saving them."},
{q:"As in the days of what place have they deeply corrupted themselves?", options:["Gibeah","Jericho","Ai","Sodom"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:9",verse:"They have deeply corrupted themselves, as in the days of Gibeah: therefore he will remember their iniquity, he will visit their sins.", insight:"Gibeah recalls the darkest outrage of the judges; God says the present corruption has sunk to that ancient depth."},
{q:"At first, what did Israel look like to God?", options:["Like grapes in the wilderness and the firstripe figs","Like thorns in the desert","Like chaff before the wind","Like a withered vine"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:10",verse:"I found Israel like grapes in the wilderness; I saw your fathers as the firstripe in the fig tree at her first time: but they went to Baal-peor, and separated themselves unto that shame; and their abominations were according as they loved.", insight:"God remembers her first sweetness with delight; grapes in the wilderness picture a love that was once fresh and surprising."},
{q:"To whom did they go, separating themselves unto shame?", options:["To Baal-peor","To the gods of Egypt","To the idols of Tyre","To the high places of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:10",verse:"I found Israel like grapes in the wilderness; I saw your fathers as the firstripe in the fig tree at her first time: but they went to Baal-peor, and separated themselves unto that shame; and their abominations were according as they loved.", insight:"From firstripe figs to Baal's shame, the fall was steep; they became as vile as the very thing they chose to love."},
{q:"Like what shall the glory of Ephraim fly away?", options:["Like a bird","Like smoke","Like a shadow","Like a dream"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:11",verse:"As for Ephraim, their glory shall fly away like a bird, from the birth, and from the womb, and from the conception.", insight:"Glory takes wing and does not return; the bird image shows blessing departing swiftly and completely."},
{q:"From where shall that glory fly away?", options:["From the birth, and from the womb, and from the conception","From the temple, and from the altar, and from the priest","From the city, and from the field, and from the vineyard","From the king, and from the princes, and from the judges"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:11",verse:"As for Ephraim, their glory shall fly away like a bird, from the birth, and from the womb, and from the conception.", insight:"The loss reaches back to life's very beginnings; judgment touches birth, womb, and conception, leaving no generation untouched."},
{q:"Though they bring up their children, what will God do?", options:["Bereave them, so that not a man is left","Make them mighty in battle","Give them lands in Canaan","Set them as rulers over nations"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:12",verse:"Though they bring up their children, yet will I bereave them, that there shall not be a man left: yea, woe also to them when I depart from them!", insight:"Children raised will be taken; the verse shows judgment striking the future itself, not only the present."},
{q:"To whom shall Ephraim bring forth his children?", options:["To the murderer","To the king's court","To the temple school","To the merchants of Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:13",verse:"Ephraim, as I saw Tyrus, is planted in a pleasant place: but Ephraim shall bring forth his children to the murderer.", insight:"Planted in a pleasant place like Tyre, Ephraim's sons are handed to the killer; prosperity without God ends in handing the next generation to ruin."},
{q:"What does Hosea ask the LORD to give them?", options:["A miscarrying womb and dry breasts","Fields of wheat and vats of oil","Sons like olive plants","Daughters like corner stones"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:14",verse:"Give them, O LORD: what wilt thou give? give them a miscarrying womb and dry breasts.", insight:"The prophet asks for barrenness as mercy; better no children, he pleads, than children raised to be devoured by judgment."},
{q:"Where is all their wickedness, and what will God do about His house?", options:["In Gilgal; He will drive them out of His house and love them no more","In Bethel; He will shut its gates forever","In Dan; He will burn its altars","In Samaria; He will break its walls"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 9:15",verse:"All their wickedness is in Gilgal: for there I hated them: for the wickedness of their doings I will drive them out of mine house, I will love them no more: all their princes are revolters.", insight:"Gilgal, once the camp of covenant renewal, became the seat of wickedness; God answers by withdrawing His house and His love."}
]},
{n:10, name:"Break Up Your Fallow Ground", seal:{ref:"Hosea 10:12",verse:"Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."}, facts:["Did you know? Hosea 10:5 calls them 'the calves of Beth-aven'; Hosea renames Bethel, 'house of God,' as Beth-aven, 'house of wickedness,' because of its golden calf. (Hosea 10:5)","Did you know? 'They shall say to the mountains, Cover us' (10:8) is quoted by Jesus in Luke 23:30 and echoed in Revelation 6:16, a picture of terror at the end of the world. (Hosea 10:8)","Did you know? 'Break up your fallow ground' (10:12) borrows from farming: fallow land is hard soil left unplanted, and only the plow can open it for seed. (Hosea 10:12)"], questions:[
{q:"Israel is pictured as what kind of vine?", options:["An empty vine, bringing forth fruit unto himself","A fruitful vine planted by the rivers","A wild vine of the mountains","A tender vine in the king's garden"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:1",verse:"Israel is an empty vine, he bringeth forth fruit unto himself: according to the multitude of his fruit he hath increased the altars; according to the goodness of his land they have made goodly images.", insight:"Empty of God but full of self, the vine fruited for its own appetite; plenty spent on self is the picture of barrenness."},
{q:"According to the multitude of his fruit, what did Israel do?", options:["Increased the altars and made goodly images","Built storehouses for the poor","Sent gifts to the temple","Planted vineyards for the Levites"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:1",verse:"Israel is an empty vine, he bringeth forth fruit unto himself: according to the multitude of his fruit he hath increased the altars; according to the goodness of his land they have made goodly images.", insight:"Blessing became the budget for idolatry; the more God gave, the more altars they raised to other gods."},
{q:"What is wrong with their heart?", options:["Their heart is divided; now shall they be found faulty","Their heart is hardened beyond feeling","Their heart is empty of all thought","Their heart is broken with sorrow"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:2",verse:"Their heart is divided; now shall they be found faulty: he shall break down their altars, he shall spoil their images.", insight:"A divided heart tries to serve God and idols at once; God calls the split itself the fault, before naming any deed."},
{q:"Why do the people say they have no king?", options:["Because we feared not the LORD; what then should a king do to us?","Because the king has fled to Egypt","Because Assyria has taken him captive","Because the people refused to crown him"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:3",verse:"For now they shall say, We have no king, because we feared not the LORD; what then should a king do to us?", insight:"They confess the root: no fear of God made every king useless; a throne cannot fix what reverence alone can mend."},
{q:"What have they spoken, swearing falsely in making a covenant?", options:["Words, empty and false","Truth, kept with care","Prayers, offered in faith","Vows, sealed with sacrifice"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:4",verse:"They have spoken words, swearing falsely in making a covenant: thus judgment springeth up as hemlock in the furrows of the field.", insight:"Covenants of mere words rot the land; the verse shows that false oaths poison justice like weeds in a field."},
{q:"Judgment is said to spring up as what in the furrows of the field?", options:["As hemlock","As wheat","As thistles only","As wild grapes"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:4",verse:"They have spoken words, swearing falsely in making a covenant: thus judgment springeth up as hemlock in the furrows of the field.", insight:"Hemlock is poison among the crops; false dealing grows a bitter harvest right in the furrows meant for bread."},
{q:"Why shall the inhabitants of Samaria fear?", options:["Because of the calves of Beth-aven","Because of the coming famine","Because of the plague in the city","Because of the earthquake at night"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:5",verse:"The inhabitants of Samaria shall fear because of the calves of Beth-aven: for the people thereof shall mourn over it, and the priests thereof that rejoiced on it, for the glory thereof, because it is departed from it.", insight:"Their trusted calves become their terror; the idols they boasted in will be the reason for their dread."},
{q:"Why do the people and priests mourn over the calf?", options:["For the glory thereof, because it is departed from it","Because the gold has been stolen","Because the priests have been slain","Because the temple is shut"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:5",verse:"The inhabitants of Samaria shall fear because of the calves of Beth-aven: for the people thereof shall mourn over it, and the priests thereof that rejoiced on it, for the glory thereof, because it is departed from it.", insight:"They weep for a departing idol, not for departing God; the mourning shows where their glory truly lay."},
{q:"Where shall the calf be carried, and for whom?", options:["To Assyria, for a present to king Jareb","To Egypt, for a gift to Pharaoh","To Babylon, for tribute to the king","To Tyre, for payment of a debt"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:6",verse:"It shall be also carried unto Assyria for a present to king Jareb: Ephraim shall receive shame, and Israel shall be ashamed of his own counsel.", insight:"The god of Samaria ends as plunder for Assyria; idols finish as tribute, carried off to decorate a conqueror's court."},
{q:"As for Samaria, her king is cut off as what?", options:["As the foam upon the water","As a tree cut by the axe","As a lamp gone out","As a stone cast down"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:7",verse:"As for Samaria, her king is cut off as the foam upon the water.", insight:"Foam on water vanishes without a trace; the king's power, once feared, dissolves like bubbles on a stream."},
{q:"What shall come up on their altars when the high places are destroyed?", options:["The thorn and the thistle","The wheat and the barley","The lily and the rose","The olive and the fig"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:8",verse:"The high places also of Aven, the sin of Israel, shall be destroyed: the thorn and the thistle shall come up on their altars; and they shall say to the mountains, Cover us; and to the hills, Fall on us.", insight:"Weeds reclaim the altars of idolatry; where false worship stood, only thorns will grow to mark the ruin."},
{q:"What will the people say to the mountains and the hills?", options:["Cover us; and, Fall on us","Save us; and, Hide our gold","Help us; and, Bring us water","Lead us; and, Show the way"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:8",verse:"The high places also of Aven, the sin of Israel, shall be destroyed: the thorn and the thistle shall come up on their altars; and they shall say to the mountains, Cover us; and to the hills, Fall on us.", insight:"They beg the hills to bury them; terror makes men ask creation to hide them from the Creator they rejected."},
{q:"Since when has Israel sinned?", options:["From the days of Gibeah","From the days of Moses","From the days of David","From the days of Solomon"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:9",verse:"O Israel, thou hast sinned from the days of Gibeah: there they stood: the battle in Gibeah against the children of iniquity did not overtake them.", insight:"The sin is old, reaching back to Gibeah's infamy; God traces the present rebellion to a long, unbroken line."},
{q:"When shall the people be gathered against them?", options:["When they shall bind themselves in their two furrows","When the harvest is fully ripe","When the trumpet sounds in Zion","When the king calls for war"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:10",verse:"It is in my desire that I should chastise them; and the people shall be gathered against them, when they shall bind themselves in their two furrows.", insight:"Bound in their own furrows, yoked to their sin, they draw judgment on themselves; God gathers the nations against a self-bound people."},
{q:"Ephraim is pictured as what?", options:["An heifer that is taught, and loveth to tread out the corn","A wild ox that breaks the yoke","A lamb led to the slaughter","A horse prepared for battle"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:11",verse:"And Ephraim is as an heifer that is taught, and loveth to tread out the corn; but I passed over upon her fair neck: I will make Ephraim to ride; Judah shall plow, and Jacob shall break his clods.", insight:"The trained heifer loved the easy work of treading grain; Israel loved blessing without the harder labor of obedience."},
{q:"In the yoke of judgment, who shall plow and who shall break the clods?", options:["Judah shall plow, and Jacob shall break his clods","Ephraim shall plow, and Manasseh shall sow","Israel shall plow, and Judah shall reap","Samaria shall plow, and Jerusalem shall gather"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:11",verse:"And Ephraim is as an heifer that is taught, and loveth to tread out the corn; but I passed over upon her fair neck: I will make Ephraim to ride; Judah shall plow, and Jacob shall break his clods.", insight:"Easy treading gives way to hard plowing; God puts the pampered heifer to the stern work of breaking up hard ground."},
{q:"What are they commanded to sow and to reap?", options:["Sow in righteousness, reap in mercy","Sow in the field, reap in the barn","Sow with tears, reap with singing","Sow in spring, reap in summer"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:12",verse:"Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you.", insight:"Righteousness sown returns as mercy reaped; the verse ties the harvest of kindness to the seed of right living."},
{q:"How long is it time to seek the LORD?", options:["Till He come and rain righteousness upon you","Till the evening sacrifice is offered","Till the seventh year of release","Till the king returns from battle"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:12",verse:"Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you.", insight:"Seeking is to continue until God Himself comes; the rain of righteousness is worth waiting and working for."},
{q:"Why have they eaten the fruit of lies?", options:["Because thou didst trust in thy way, in the multitude of thy mighty men","Because the prophets spoke falsely","Because the harvest failed three years","Because the priests withheld the truth"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:13",verse:"Ye have plowed wickedness, ye have reaped iniquity; ye have eaten the fruit of lies: because thou didst trust in thy way, in the multitude of thy mighty men.", insight:"Trust in self and soldiers bears the fruit of lies; the verse names the twin idols of Israel's confidence."},
{q:"So shall Bethel do unto you because of your great wickedness; what shall happen to the king of Israel?", options:["In a morning shall he utterly be cut off","In a year shall he be restored","In battle shall he be honored","In peace shall he grow old"], a:0, ref:"Hosea 10:15",verse:"So shall Bethel do unto you because of your great wickedness: in a morning shall the king of Israel utterly be cut off.", insight:"Bethel, the seat of the calf, becomes the seat of ruin; the king trusted in wickedness is cut off in a single morning."}
]}
]);
